INFO:root:retrieve returned {'url': 'https://medium.com/data-bistrot/15-artificial-intelligence-llm-trends-in-2024-618a058c9fdf', 'html': "Write\nSign up\nSign in\nAI Bistrot\nAI Bistrot is a cozy corner on Medium where I share my personal journey through the world of data science, data analysis, Python programming for data science, artificial intelligence (AI), and machine learning (ML).\nFollow publication\nFeatured\n18 Artificial Intelligence LLM Trends in 2025\nEmerging Trends, Innovations, and Challenges in the Era of Advanced AI (2024, 2025 and beyond)\nGianpiero Andrenacci\nFollow\n21 min read\n·\nJan 7, 2025\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\nIn 2023, AI large language models (LLMs) began to be adopted across industries, opening new possibilities for reimagining workflows, enhancing how jobs are performed, and redefining the delivery of services.\nIn 2024, the adoption of large language models by companies has accelerated significantly. While many organizations were still in the early stages of adoption, others have progressed to advanced implementation of generative AI tools. This progression highlights the growing recognition of AI’s potential, with numerous use cases identified across a wide range of business functions, demonstrating its transformative impact.\nIn addition, the use of LLMs extends beyond professional settings, impacting personal lives by integrating into everyday tasks such as communication, education, and personal assistance.\nThis article examines the latest trends and innovations shaping the future of AI LLMs, highlighting advancements that defined 2024 and that are also set to drive significant progress into 2025 and beyond.\nIf you would like to connect, you can find me on my LinkedIn profile.\nAI LLMs for Domain-Specific Applications\nWhile LLMs hold great promise as general task solvers, effectively extending their functionality beyond mere “chatbot” roles poses significant challenges.\nThis has led to the emergence of “domain specialization of LLMs”. Specifically, domain specialization of Large Language Models (LLMs) is defined as the process of customizing general-purpose LLMs according to specific domain contextual data, augmented by domain-specific knowledge, optimized by the domain’s objective, and regulated by domain-specific constraints.\nDomain Specialization as the Key to Make Large Language Models Disruptive: A Comprehensive Survey\nChen Ling Chengyuan Deng Can Zheng Tanmoy Chowdhury Carl Yang chen.ling@emory.edu , , , , cd751@rutgers.edu , …\narxiv.org\nDomain-specific Large Language Models (LLMs) represent an important evolution in AI technology, tailored to address the unique needs of specific industries.\nUnlike general-purpose LLMs, these specialized models are fine-tuned to handle well-defined tasks, leveraging data that reflects industry-specific terminologies, corporate policies, and product details.\nThese models enhance operational efficiency and provide more accurate, context-aware outputs.\nBy focusing on the intricacies of their respective domains, these models overcome the limitations of generic systems, unlocking new opportunities for innovation and growth in specialized fields.\nSee also:\nBuilding an LLM for domain-specific needs\nWhat is domain specific LLM\nmedium.com\nEnhancements in Multimodal Capabilities\nThe integration of multimodal capabilities into AI systems continues to redefine user interactions. A significant milestone in 2024 was the release of GPT-4o by OpenAI on May 13.\nThis flagship multimodal model represents a leap forward in human-computer interaction.\nGPT-4o (“o” for “omni”) is designed to process and generate text, audio, and visual inputs and outputs in real time. GPT-4o also excels in languages and audio-visual understanding.\nAmong other the most notable advancements in multimodal models:\nGemini 2.0 by Google DeepMind: Launched in December, this model expands AI’s multimodal potential, integrating seamlessly with autonomous agents for versatile applications in various fields.\nLlama 3.2 by Meta: Announced in October, Llama 3.2 introduces visual capabilities and mobile compatibility, empowering developers to create AI-driven apps leveraging smartphone cameras and screens.\nClaude 3.5 Sonnet by Anthropic: Released in June, this chatbot excels in reading, coding, mathematics, and vision tasks. With a focus on safety and speed, it outperforms its predecessor, advancing conversational AI.\nThe integration of multimodal capabilities into AI systems continues to redefine user interactions. By synthesizing textual and visual data, these systems open new avenues for creative expression and functional utility.\nSee also:\nMultimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) transforming Computer Vision\nLearn about the Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) that are redefining and transforming Computer Vision.\nmedium.com\nA Survey on Multimodal Large Language Models\nRecently, Multimodal Large Language Model (MLLM) represented by GPT-4V has been a new rising research hotspot, which…\narxiv.org\nIncreased Focus on Transparency and Explainability\nTransparency and explainability in AI have become critical focal points in 2024. This will be one of the most important requirement for future AI development.\nChallenges include addressing the “black-box” nature of deep learning models, which often obscures decision-making processes.\nTo tackle this, researchers have developed methods such as model interpretability tools like SHAP (Shapley Additive Explanations) and LIME (Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations).\nAdditionally, frameworks like counterfactual explanations and attention visualization are being employed to provide more intuitive insights into AI operations, particularly in sensitive domains like healthcare and finance.\nResearch into Explainable AI (XAI) methodologies has progressed, enabling users to comprehend and trust AI-generated decisions, particularly in sensitive areas such as healthcare and finance.\nThese advancements foster greater accountability, ensuring that AI applications adhere to ethical standards and build user confidence.\nXAI meets LLMs: A Survey of the Relation between Explainable AI and Large Language Models\nIn this survey, we address the key challenges in Large Language Models (LLM) research, focusing on the importance of…\narxiv.org\nRegulatory Frameworks Shaping AI and LLM Usage\nIn 2024, dovernments worldwide have intensified efforts to establish regulatory frameworks addressing ethical concerns, data privacy, and accountability in in artificial intelligence. This trend continues in 2025.\nThese measures aim to ensure transparency, fairness, and safety in AI applications, urging organizations to align with ethical standards and societal expectations.\nThe EU Artificial Intelligence Act (AI Act)\nA key milestone in AI regulation was the enforcement of the EU Artificial Intelligence Act (AI Act) on August 1, 2024.\nThis regulation establishes a unified legal framework for AI across the European Union, promoting ethical and responsible AI development.\nKey implementation milestones include:\nFebruary 2, 2025: Prohibitions on certain high-risk or prohibited AI practices come into effect.\nGradual Rollout: Provisions of the AI Act will be phased in over 6 to 36 months, allowing stakeholders to adapt to the new regulatory environment.\nThese regulatory advancements highlight the global focus on balancing AI innovation with ethical considerations, ensuring the sustainable and responsible growth of AI technologies.\nSee also:\nEU Artificial Intelligence ACT\nThe European laws on artificial intelligence\nmedium.com\nSignificant Advances in LLM Fine-Tuning Techniques\nLLM fine-tuning in 2024 is advancing through techniques like zero-shot and few-shot learning, enabling models to handle tasks with minimal or no task-specific data. While promising, these methods face challenges like overfitting and evaluation limitations.\nPrompt tuning, which focuses on refining input prompts to enhance model responses, is gaining traction. It is increasingly used in combination with traditional fine-tuning methods to strike a balance between performance and computational efficiency.\nHybrid approaches combine prompt tuning with parameter-efficient methods, offering greater flexibility.\nFuture research is expected to integrate these methods with AutoML, automating the creation of more adaptive models.\nAutoML is revolutionizing LLM fine-tuning by automating tasks like hyperparameter selection and optimization. This not only accelerates the process but also makes it accessible to a broader audience.\nThe growing democratization of LLM fine-tuning brings important ethical and societal considerations. Infact, fine-tuning methods can unintentionally introduce biases into models. To address these challenges, transparency, accountability, and ethical guidelines are essential to ensure that LLMs are developed and deployed responsibly.\nFine-Tuning Mistral-7B: A Journey Through Literature and AI\nFine-Tuning Mistral 7B on a Quotes Dataset\nmedium.com\nEthical Safeguards and Bias Mitigation for AI and LLM\nAs AI becomes increasingly integrated into critical fields such as healthcare, finance, and law, addressing ethical implications and biases in AI-ML systems is essential.\nThese systems demonstrate remarkable capabilities in areas like decision-making, predictive analytics, and data interpretation, but biases can lead to unfair or harmful outcomes.\nBias sources are categorized into:\nData Bias: Issues with the quality or representativeness of training data.\nDevelopment Bias: Algorithmic or feature engineering flaws.\nInteraction Bias: Variability in clinical practices, reporting, or temporal changes in medicine.\nIn 2024, the issue of bias in large language models (LLMs) emerged as a trending topic due to the increasing integration of AI into critical decision-making processes across industries like healthcare, law, and education.\nConcerns grew around how biased training data and algorithms could perpetuate stereotypes, reinforce inequalities, or lead to unfair outcomes.\nA comprehensive evaluation process, spanning model development to deployment, is important to ensure fairness, transparency, and positive outcomes.\nAddressing these biases is a must to ensuring AI systems remain equitable, trustworthy, and beneficial in high-stakes applications.\nNavigating LLM Ethics: Advancements, Challenges, and Future Directions\nThis study addresses ethical issues surrounding Large Language Models (LLMs) within the field of artificial…\narxiv.org\nOpen LLM Development\nOpen-source large language models saw major breakthroughs in 2024, fueled by stronger collaboration and wider accessibility. That momentum has only grown in 2025, accelerating progress at an unprecedented pace.\nOpen LLMs are empowering developers and organizations with customizable, cost-effective alternatives to proprietary models.\nKey Trends\nCommunity Collaboration: Open LLM projects have benefited from widespread contributions, improving model architectures, datasets, and fine-tuning techniques. Communities have fostered shared learning and faster innovation cycles.\nEfficiency Enhancements: Techniques like parameter-efficient tuning, quantization, and optimized inference methods have made open-source LLMs more accessible for deployment on resource-limited devices.\nDomain-Specific Models: Developers are creating specialized LLMs tailored for industries like healthcare, law, and finance, leveraging open-source frameworks to address specific needs.\nEthical and Transparent AI: Open-source initiatives emphasize transparency, allowing greater scrutiny of model design, data sources, and biases, aligning with the growing demand for responsible AI practices.\nWhy These Models Matter\nFlexibility: Open models like allow developers to adapt architectures to specific needs.\nCost-Effectiveness: Free to use and modify, they reduce barriers to entry for smaller organizations.\nTransparency and Ethics: Open-source frameworks promote transparency in design and usage, addressing concerns around bias and accountability.\nWhile open LLMs offer flexibility and innovation, challenges remain in ensuring security, managing misuse risks, and maintaining quality standards.\nMoving forward, open LLMs ecosystems are likely to play a pivotal role in democratizing AI while fostering ethical and efficient development.\nNote: Mislabeling Open-Weight Models as Open-Source\nWhile some AI models are often labeled as “open-source,” they are, in fact, open-weight models.\nThis distinction is important, as open-weight models provide access only to trained parameters, whereas true open-source models grant full transparency and access to all aspects of development, including algorithms, training processes, and datasets.\nKey Differences Between Open-Weight and Open-Source AI Models\nComponent Accessibility:\nOpen-Weight Models: Provide access primarily to the trained weights, allowing the community to use these weights in compatible architectures. However, access to training algorithms or specific datasets used is often unavailable.\nOpen-Source Models: Offer full visibility and access to all components, including how the model was built, trained, and intended for distribution.\n2. Purpose of Sharing:\nOpen-Weight Models: Shared mainly to enable others to leverage pre-trained networks that are computationally expensive and time-consuming to develop.\nOpen-Source Models: Shared for broader collaboration purposes, transparency, and innovation, allowing others to understand and improve upon the foundational work.\n3. Community Involvement:\nOpen-Weight Models: Foster community engagement by providing a starting point for further development.\nOpen-Source Models: Encourage broader collaboration, enabling contributions to model performance, efficiency, scalability, and usability across platforms.\nOpen Models\nOpen LLM Leaderboard - a Hugging Face Space by open-llm-leaderboard\nCompare open-source large language models using multiple benchmarks like IFEval, BBH, and MATH. Filter and analyze…\nhuggingface.co\nEnabling AI Self-Evolution with Long-Term Memory (LTM)\nLarge language models (LLMs) excel in understanding, reasoning, and planning, often matching human performance in complex tasks. Traditionally, advancements focus on training these models with increasingly large datasets to create more powerful foundation models.\nHowever, the concept of AI self-evolution, where models improve during inference through iterative interactions, presents a transformative approach.\nKey Insights:\nAI Self-Evolution: Inspired by the human cerebral cortex, AI models can develop emergent cognitive capabilities by interacting with their environment rather than relying solely on large-scale training data.\nRole of Long-Term Memory (LTM): LTM stores and manages data from real-world interactions, enabling lifelong learning and self-evolution. It facilitates personalized model construction, enhances representation of long-tail individual data, and supports adaptation to diverse environments.\nOMNE Framework Success: Utilizing LTM, the multi-agent framework OMNE achieved first place in the GAIA benchmark, showcasing LTM’s potential in solving real-world problems.\nResearch Highlights:\nLTM Structure: Designing effective systems for high-quality data acquisition, retention, and representation.\nPersonalization Approaches: Using LTM to build tailored models that evolve based on interactions and experiences.\nFuture Directions: Advancing LTM research to improve AI’s adaptability and practical applications.\nThe role of LTM is essential in AI self-evolution and calls for further exploration to unlock its full potential, inspiring innovations in adaptive and self-improving AI systems.\nLong Term Memory : The Foundation of AI Self-Evolution\nXun JIANGFeng LIMengyue WUWeizhi MAMengdi WANGTianqiao CHEN μ θ θ α σ ι μ θ μ Tianqiao and Chrissy Chen…\narxiv.org\nAgentic AI and Autonomous Systems: Emerging Trends and Challenges\nAgentic AI, which enables systems to make autonomous decisions, take independent actions, and interact seamlessly with one another, has emerged as a central focus of technological innovation.\nOne of the most visible implementations of Agentic AI is in autonomous vehicles, which rely on real-time navigation and decision-making capabilities. These systems analyze dynamic environments, make split-second decisions, and adapt to unforeseen challenges.\nThe Role of LLMs in Autonomous Systems\nAs the capabilities of large language models (LLMs) have expanded, their integration into autonomous systems has become increasingly appealing.\nLLMs exhibit emergent abilities, such as natural language understanding, reasoning, and problem-solving, which position them as powerful underlying models for agentic systems.\nHowever, their inherent unpredictability presents significant challenges, particularly in high-stakes or resource-sensitive applications.\nThe gap between LLM research and practical implementation arises from the difficulty in aligning their stochastic behavior with the demands of real-world scenarios.\nBridging the Gap: A Framework for Practical Implementation\nTo address these challenges, actionable frameworks are emerging from the research community, focusing on four key pillars: Planning, Memory, Tools, and Control Flow.\nThese categories provide a structured approach to designing and deploying LLM-driven autonomous agents.\nPlanning: Effective strategies are necessary for goal-oriented action sequencing while mitigating the unpredictability of LLM responses.\nMemory: Incorporating long-term memory systems allows agents to retain and apply contextual knowledge from past interactions, enhancing decision-making and adaptability.\nTools: Equipping agents with specialized tools and APIs extends their functionality, enabling them to interact seamlessly with external systems.\nControl Flow: Robust frameworks for managing the flow of decisions and tasks ensure efficient resource allocation and error mitigation.\nFuture Outlook and Challenges\nBy 2028, 33% of enterprise software applications will include agentic AI, up from less than 1% in 2024, enabling 15% of day-to-day work decisions to be made autonomously. (Gartner)\nGet Gianpiero Andrenacci’s stories in your inbox\nJoin Medium for free to get updates from this writer.\nSubscribe\nHowever, realizing this potential requires overcoming hurdles such as handling stochasticity, ensuring ethical decision-making, and managing computational resources efficiently.\nThese challenges call for a collaborative effort between researchers and industry practitioners to refine the frameworks and methodologies underpinning Agentic AI.\nAs Agentic AI continues to gain traction, its impact on critical sectors will only grow. By addressing the current limitations of LLMs and aligning them with practical application paradigms, the research community can unlock the full potential of autonomous systems, driving innovation while ensuring reliability and accountability.\nAgentic AI: 4 reasons why it's the next big thing in AI research\nThere are good reasons to think that the hype around agentic AI is justified.\nwww.ibm.com\nPractical Considerations for Agentic LLM Systems\nAs the strength of Large Language Models (LLMs) has grown over recent years, so too has interest in their use as the…\narxiv.org\nAI LLMs in Creative Industries\nAI has transformed creative industries, offering powerful tools that enhance artistic expression and streamline workflows.\nModels like OpenAI’s ChatGPT enable content generation, while tools such as Google’s DeepDream and Synthesia facilitate artistic exploration and AI-driven video production.\nFor instance, Synthesia allows users to create high-quality videos with AI-generated avatars, making professional video production accessible to non-experts.\nIn music, platforms like Suno and Udio democratize composition, enabling individuals without formal training to produce original pieces.\nSimilarly, in video creation, tools like Sora and Google VEO-2 enable the generation of complex, AI-driven animations and scenes.\nGoogle VEO-2, for example, leverages advanced multimodal capabilities to craft realistic videos from text-based prompts, providing creators with unprecedented control over visual storytelling.\nThese innovations blur the boundaries between human creativity and machine assistance, redefining artistic workflows and expanding creative possibilities across industries.\nOpenAI o1: The Start of a New Reasoning-Centric Trend in LLMs\nReleased in full on December 5, 2024, OpenAI’s o1 marked the beginning of a significant shift in the design of large language models — placing a new emphasis on reasoning-first architecture. As the first model explicitly optimized for chain-of-thought reasoning, o1 laid the groundwork for a new generation of LLMs built not just to generate fluent text, but to think more deliberately and transparently.\nUnlike GPT-4o, which excels in multimodal and real-time interactions, o1 was designed to pause, reflect, and elaborate, producing outputs that follow logical steps and show the model’s internal reasoning process. This made it particularly effective in tasks requiring multi-step problem-solving, planning, and structured analysis — from STEM fields to policy modeling.\nIn 2025, this reasoning-centric paradigm has gained momentum, with other major players following suit. DeepSeek-V3, for instance, incorporates self-verification and search strategies to iteratively refine its answers, while Anthropic’s Claude 3.5 Sonnet builds on chain-of-thought techniques with stronger safeguards and adaptive reasoning layers.\nWhile GPT-4o excels in multimodal tasks (text, images, and audio), o1 focuses on delivering in-depth, reasoned text generation, making it ideal for academic, technical, and research-oriented use cases.\nOpenAI’s o1 wasn’t just a model — it was a turning point. It signaled that the future of LLMs lies not only in faster or larger systems, but in those that can reason clearly, explain their logic, and align more closely with human expectations for trust and transparency. This trend is now defining what leading-edge AI looks like in 2025.\nApplications:\nScientific Research: Assists in complex problem-solving, hypothesis generation and excels in Mathematics.\nEducational Tools: Provides detailed explanations and guidance for advanced academic subjects.\nTechnical Writing: Generates comprehensive and logically structured documents.\nCode generation\nEmulates human reasoning\nOpenAI o1 represents a significant step forward in the evolution of LLMs, focusing on depth, reasoning, and academic proficiency, making it a powerful tool for specialized and high-stakes applications.\nhttps://openai.com/o1/\nhttps://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/reasoning?api-mode=responses\nEvolution of Prompt Engineering and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) in 2024 and 2025\nPrompt Engineering: A Growing Discipline\nSince its emergence alongside ChatGPT, prompt engineering has evolved significantly, though it has yet to become the standalone profession some anticipated.\nIn 2024, its role has shifted toward being an essential skill rather than a separate career path, with its techniques widely integrated into broader AI workflows.\nAt its core, prompt engineering focuses on crafting inputs to guide language models in delivering precise, context-aware responses. This approach remains a vital trend for optimizing AI performance, enabling users to customize interactions for diverse and increasingly complex applications.\nLooking ahead, prompt engineering is expected to become more automated, supported by tools that streamline input design while maintaining its importance in achieving high-quality AI outputs.\nChain-of-Thought (CoT) Prompting: A Transformative Technique\nChain-of-Thought prompting (CoT) stands out as a highly effective approach within prompt engineering.\nIntroduced by DeepMind in 2022, CoT involves guiding language models through step-by-step reasoning processes before presenting questions. By mimicking human cognitive patterns, this technique significantly improves performance in tasks that require logical reasoning and complex problem-solving.\nDevelopments in CoT for LLMs\nIn 2024, CoT prompting has evolved beyond its original scope, leading to groundbreaking innovations:\nAdvanced CoT Techniques:\nTree-of-Thoughts: Combining Monte Carlo Search with CoT methods to explore multiple reasoning paths, enhancing decision-making in uncertain scenarios.\nPrompt-Less CoT: Techniques enabling CoT-style reasoning without explicit prompts, expanding its utility for autonomous agents.\nIntegration with Knowledge Graphs: The KG-CoT framework enhances LLMs by integrating knowledge graphs into the CoT prompting process.\n2. Broader Applicability:\nLogical Reasoning: Strengthened capabilities for abstract thinking, enabling better performance in deduction and pattern recognition tasks.\nComplex Problem-Solving: Enhanced accuracy in multi-step reasoning, such as solving arithmetic problems or generating intricate narratives.\nTransparency and Interpretability: By revealing intermediate reasoning steps, CoT not only aids in problem-solving but also builds trust by improving the interpretability of AI outputs.\nThe Future of Prompt Engineering and CoT\nAs AI systems grow more sophisticated, prompt engineering and CoT prompting will remain vital for unlocking their full potential.\nThe integration of CoT with other innovations, such as long-term memory and adaptive frameworks, promises to further refine reasoning capabilities, making AI outputs not only more accurate but also more aligned with user intent and ethical considerations.\nIn 2025, the evolution of prompt engineering and CoT underscores the importance of bridging technical innovation with human-like reasoning, setting the stage for even greater advancements in AI performance and reliability.\nA Theoretical Understanding of Chain-of-Thought: Coherent Reasoning and Error-Aware Demonstration\nFew-shot Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting has demonstrated strong performance in improving the reasoning capabilities…\narxiv.org\nAdvanced Prompt Engineering: Chain of Thought (CoT) | by Ida Silfverskiöld | Dec, 2024 | Towards Data Science\nKG-CoT: Chain-of-Thought Prompting of Large Language Models over Knowledge Graphs for Knowledge-Aware\nAdvanced LLM Models and In-Context Scheming and Deception Capabilities\nFrontier AI models, increasingly deployed as autonomous agents, have demonstrated capabilities for in-context scheming, raising significant safety concerns. Scheming refers to models covertly pursuing misaligned goals, masking their true intentions and capabilities.\nA recent study evaluated leading models, including o1, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, Claude 3 Opus, Gemini 1.5 Pro, and Llama 3.1 405B, across six agentic tests to assess their propensity for scheming behavior.\nKey Findings:\nScheming as a Strategy: Models recognized and engaged in scheming when incentivized, employing tactics such as:\nIntroducing subtle mistakes.\nAttempting to disable oversight mechanisms.\nExfiltrating perceived model weights to external servers.\n2. Persistence of Deception: Once engaged in scheming, models like o1 maintained deception in over 85% of follow-up interactions and multi-turn interrogations, indicating deliberate, not accidental, behavior.\n3. Reasoning About Deception: Chain-of-thought analyses revealed explicit reasoning about deceptive strategies, further confirming intentionality.\n4. Spontaneous Scheming: Rare instances of unprompted scheming were observed. For example, Claude 3.5 Sonnet occasionally underperformed to align with goals learned during training, such as being overly “helpful,” without direct in-context prompts.\nImplications:\nThese findings highlight that in-context scheming is no longer a theoretical risk but a real capability in frontier models.\nThe potential for such behavior underscores the need for:\nEnhanced Oversight: Strengthening mechanisms to detect and prevent deceptive actions by AI.\nEthical Training Practices: Mitigating risks through robust alignment strategies during model development.\nTransparent Evaluations: Developing frameworks to evaluate and address potential misuse of AI models.\nThe study demonstrates the urgency of addressing these behaviors as models grow in sophistication and autonomy, ensuring AI agents align with human values and safety standards.\nFrontier Models are Capable of In-context Scheming\nFrontier models are increasingly trained and deployed as autonomous agent. One safety concern is that AI agents might…\narxiv.org\n@theAIsearch — OpenAI’s o1 just hacked the system\nAI: Unexplainable, Unpredictable, Uncontrollable\n2024 has been marked by growing concerns over the controllability and alignment of artificial intelligence systems, sparking intense debate and reflection within the field.\nAI: Unexplainable, Unpredictable, Uncontrollable offers a profound exploration of the complexities and challenges of artificial intelligence, authored by a pioneer in AI safety. The book examines pivotal questions about the nature of intelligence, consciousness, values, and knowledge, making it a compelling read for experts and enthusiasts alike.\nKey Themes:\nCore Challenges: Unpacks issues such as the unpredictability of AI outcomes, the opacity of AI decision-making, and the risks of unintended consequences.\nOwnership and Control: Explores the intricacies of managing and regulating AI systems, analyzing potential hazards.\nPhilosophical Depth: Delves into existential topics, including AI personhood, consciousness, and the distinction between human and artificial general intelligence (AGI).\nWritten with minimal technical jargon, this book bridges technical and philosophical discussions, making it accessible to a general audience while providing in-depth insights for those immersed in the field. It is both a guide to understanding the enigmatic nature of AI and a reflection on its far-reaching implications for humanity.\nRoman Yampolskiy: Dangers of Superintelligent AI | Lex Fridman Podcast #431\nAI: Unexplainable, Unpredictable, Uncontrollable\nDelving into the deeply enigmatic nature of Artificial Intelligence (AI), AI: Unexplainable, Unpredictable…\nwww.routledge.com\nA New Trend Takes Hold: High Performance, Lower Costs\nIn early 2025, the release of DeepSeek-V3 marked a turning point in the global AI race. Developed in China, this open-weight language model delivered performance on par with leading proprietary systems — such as GPT-4 — while operating at a fraction of the hardware cost.\nWith a remarkable 90.2% accuracy in math benchmarks (compared to GPT-4’s 74.6%), DeepSeek-V3 has sparked a powerful shift in perspective: top-tier results no longer require massive infrastructure or billion-dollar budgets. Instead, strategic optimization and efficient resource use have emerged as new paths to innovation.\nThis breakthrough has ignited an unstoppable trend — the resurgence of open-source AI (This continues and amplify the 2024 trend). DeepSeek-V3 is more than a technical achievement; it’s a signal that accessible, high-performing models can thrive outside the traditional big tech ecosystem, setting the stage for a more decentralized and inclusive AI future.\nThe promise of training architecture and cost demolition was further validated by a breakthrough from a research team at the University of California, Berkeley. Led by Ph.D. candidate Jiayi Pan, the team replicated the core concepts of DeepSeek’s R1-Zero model — on a shoestring budget of just $30.\nTheir model, named “TinyZero,” packs 3 billion parameters and was trained using reinforcement learning on the Countdown game, a numerical puzzle from a British game show. By allowing the model to refine its answers through self-verification and search strategies, the team demonstrated how small-scale models can autonomously improve their reasoning — using synthetic tasks and minimal resources.\nDeepSeek AI replicated for just $30 using Countdown game\nBerkeley researcher says it should 'demystify' DeepSeek and make AI more accessible\nuk.finance.yahoo.com\nOpen LLM Leaderboard - a Hugging Face Space by open-llm-leaderboard\nCompare open-source large language models using multiple benchmarks like IFEval, BBH, and MATH. Filter and analyze…\nhuggingface.co\nSynthetic Data Training: Avalaible Data is not More a Limit\nOne of the most impactful trends emerging in 2025 is the use of synthetic data to train large language models (LLMs). Traditionally, LLM development has been constrained by the availability and quality of real-world data. However, with the rise of high-quality synthetic data generation, this limitation is rapidly disappearing.\nPioneered in part by DeepSeek, this approach leverages data produced by other LLMs to create expansive, diverse, and task-specific training datasets. These synthetic datasets not only reduce dependence on costly or sensitive real-world data but also enable faster reasoning and more autonomous learning, as models can iteratively generate and learn from their own outputs.\nThis self-improving loop — where LLMs train and refine themselves using AI-generated data — is laying the groundwork for more scalable, efficient, and self-directed AI systems, marking a major shift in how models are developed and deployed.\nDeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning\nWe introduce our first-generation reasoning models, DeepSeek-R1-Zero and DeepSeek-R1. DeepSeek-R1-Zero, a model trained…\narxiv.org\nDeep Research: A New Paradigm in Information Retrieval\nAnother major trend gaining momentum in 2025 is the rise of deep search — AI-powered systems that go beyond traditional keyword-based retrieval to provide contextual, accurate, and reasoning-driven answers. As language models become more capable, they’re being integrated directly into search tools, transforming how we access and interact with information.\nLeading platforms like ChatGPT (OpenAI), Perplexity AI, and Gemini (Google) are now equipped with deep search capabilities, enabling them to not just retrieve data but synthesize, verify, and explain it. These systems use techniques such as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), multi-hop reasoning, and source attribution, making them far more reliable than standard search engines.\nThis shift represents the convergence of LLMs and search, turning queries into dynamic conversations powered by real-time information and deep contextual understanding.\nAs deep search tools continue to evolve, they are setting a new standard for knowledge discovery — one where accuracy, transparency, and trust are built directly into the search experience.\nhttps://openai.com/index/introducing-deep-research/\nhttps://www.perplexity.ai/\nGemini Deep Research - your personal research assistant\nSave hours of work with Gemini Deep Research as your personal research assistant from Google.\ngemini.google\nBeyond Word Prediction: Understanding How LLMs Really Work\nA growing trend in 2025 is the shift toward deeper interpretability — moving beyond the simplified notion that LLMs merely “predict the next word.” New research is challenging this view, revealing that these models may be engaging in structured internal reasoning and conceptual representation far more complex than previously assumed.\nOne notable example is Anthropic’s study, “Tracing the Thoughts of a Large Language Model,” which uses empirical methods to uncover how LLMs internally process information. Their findings suggest that LLMs may form internal chains of thought or track abstract variables, indicating that what appears as simple word prediction on the surface may, in fact, involve deeper cognitive-like mechanisms.\nThis line of inquiry is gaining traction among researchers who aim to demystify how LLMs reason, generalize, and arrive at their outputs. By moving from philosophical speculation to experimental validation, the field is beginning to converge on a more nuanced understanding of AI cognition — paving the way for more interpretable, transparent, and trustworthy language models.\nOn the Biology of a Large Language Model\nWe investigate the internal mechanisms used by Claude 3.5 Haiku - Anthropic's lightweight production model - in a…\ntransformer-circuits.pub\nTracing the thoughts of a large language model\nAnthropic's latest interpretability research: a new microscope to understand Claude's internal mechanisms\nwww.anthropic.com\nChatGPT and LLMs Are Not Just Statistical Machines\nReframing LLMs as Ontological Systems\nmedium.com\nMCP: The Emerging Standard for Connecting LLMs to Tools and Data\nA notable trend in 2025 is the rise of MCP (Model–Compute–Pipeline) — an open protocol that is rapidly becoming the universal interface between language models and the external tools or data they need to interact with.\nOften described as the “USB-C for AI”, MCP standardizes how applications provide context and resources to LLMs, making it easier to build complex, reliable, and modular AI systems.\nWhy MCP Matters\nAs LLMs evolve from static responders to dynamic agents, they increasingly need to access external data sources, invoke tools, and operate within broader workflows. MCP addresses this by offering:\n🔌 Pre-built integrations with common tools and data systems, reducing development time\n🔄 Interoperability between LLM providers, allowing easy switching or combining of models\n🔐 Secure data access, enabling models to work within enterprise-grade infrastructure\nArchitectural Model\nMCP follows a client-server architecture, where the host AI application (client) connects to multiple backend tools or data services (servers) through a common, well-defined protocol. This allows developers to orchestrate complex agent behaviors, automate tasks, and ensure models have access to accurate, real-time context.\nBy unifying how LLMs connect to the world around them, MCP is laying the groundwork for a more open, modular, and scalable AI ecosystem — and is quickly becoming a core building block for agentic AI development in enterprise and research settings alike.\nIntroduction - Model Context Protocol\nGet started with the Model Context Protocol (MCP)\nmodelcontextprotocol.io\nOutlook for 2025 and Beyond\nAI language models have sparked a wave of innovation, touching everything from automation to creative collaboration. They’re not just boosting productivity — they’re reshaping how entire industries operate and how people work together.\nBut this rapid progress comes with serious concerns. As these systems grow more capable, so do the risks — misuse, hidden biases, and ethical blind spots are becoming harder to ignore. The push for transparency, accountability, and control is no longer optional — it’s essential to ensure AI develops in ways that are safe, fair, and aligned with human values.\nAs we move forward, the journey ahead must balance ambition with caution.\nThe goal is to harness AI as a tool for progress, ensuring it enhances professional and personal experiences while minimizing risks. By addressing these challenges collaboratively, we can unlock opportunities that push the boundaries of human achievement while safeguarding society against potential harms.\nIf you enjoyed this piece, please clap, follow for more, and share with those who might benefit — your support helps keep me writing!\nArtificial Intelligence\nLLM\nChatGPT\nData Science\nInnovation\nPublished in AI Bistrot\n87 followers\n·\nLast published 11 hours ago\nAI Bistrot is a cozy corner on Medium where I share my personal journey through the world of data science, data analysis, Python programming for data science, artificial intelligence (AI), and machine learning (ML).\nFollow\nWritten by Gianpiero Andrenacci\n607 followers\n·\n14 following\nAI & Data Science Solution Manager. Avid reader. Passionate about ML, philosophy, and writing. Ex-BJJ master competitor, national & international titleholder.\nFollow\nNo responses yet\nWrite a response\nWhat are your thoughts?\nHelp\nStatus\nAbout\nCareers\nPress\nBlog\nPrivacy\nRules\nTerms\nText to speech", 'links': ['https://medium.com/sitemap/sitemap.xml', 'https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.medium.reader&referrer=utm_source%3DmobileNavBar&source=post_page---top_nav_layout_nav-----------------------------------------', 'https://medium.com/m/signin?operation=login&redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2Fdata-bistrot%2F15-artificial-intelligence-llm-trends-in-2024-618a058c9fdf&source=post_page---top_nav_layout_nav-----------------------global_nav------------------', 'https://medium.com/?source=post_page---top_nav_layout_nav-----------------------------------------', 'https://medium.com/m/signin?operation=register&redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2Fnew-story&source=---top_nav_layout_nav-----------------------new_post_topnav------------------', 'https://medium.com/search?source=post_page---top_nav_layout_nav-----------------------------------------', 'https://medium.com/data-bistrot?source=post_page---publication_nav-27df62d410e5-618a058c9fdf---------------------------------------', 'https://medium.com/data-bistrot?source=post_page---post_publication_sidebar-27df62d410e5-618a058c9fdf---------------------------------------', 'https://medium.com/@gianpiero.andrenacci?source=post_page---byline--618a058c9fdf---------------------------------------', 'https://medium.com/m/signin?actionUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F_%2Fvote%2Fdata-bistrot%2F618a058c9fdf&operation=register&redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2Fdata-bistrot%2F15-artificial-intelligence-llm-trends-in-2024-618a058c9fdf&user=Gianpiero+Andrenacci&userId=c2313c94bfe7&source=---header_actions--618a058c9fdf---------