INFO:root:process_current_page returned {'url': 'https://github.com/kedbin/opencode-skills/blob/main/docs/creating-skills.md', 'html': 'Skip to content\nNavigation Menu\nAppearance settings\nPlatform\nSolutions\nResources\nOpen Source\nEnterprise\nPricing\nSearch or jump to...\nSign in\nSign up\nAppearance settings\nDismiss alert\nkedbin\n/\nopencode-skills\nPublic\nNotifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\nFork 1\nStar 22\nCode\nIssues\nPull requests\nActions\nProjects\nSecurity\nInsights\nAdditional navigation options\nFiles\n main\ndocs\ncreating-skills.md\nrelearn-ing\nskills\n.gitignore\nLICENSE\nREADME.md\nBreadcrumbs\nopencode-skills/docs\n/creating-skills.md\nLatest commit\nKedbin\nInitial commit: OpenCode Skills framework with Trello manager skill\n2 months ago\nb0990bb\n · 2 months ago\nDec 22, 2025\nHistory\nHistory\nFile metadata and controls\nPreview\nCode\nBlame\n306 lines (223 loc) · 6.95 KB\nRaw\nCreating OpenCode Skills\nThis guide walks you through creating your own OpenCode skills to extend the AI assistant\'s capabilities.\nWhat is a Skill?\nA skill is a modular package that teaches OpenCode how to interact with external services, APIs, or perform specialized tasks. When OpenCode encounters a task that matches a skill\'s description, it automatically uses the skill\'s tools to accomplish the goal.\nSkill Anatomy\nEvery skill consists of these components:\nskills/\n└── your-skill-name/\n    ├── SKILL.md              # Required: Skill definition and documentation\n    └── scripts/\n        ├── main_script.py    # Your executable script(s)\n        ├── .env.example      # Required: Template for environment variables\n        └── requirements.txt  # Optional: Dependencies\nCreating Your First Skill\nStep 1: Create the Directory Structure\nmkdir -p skills/my-api-skill/scripts\nStep 2: Write the SKILL.md File\nThe SKILL.md file is the most important part - it tells OpenCode what your skill does and how to use it.\n---\nname: my-api-skill\ndescription: Brief description of what this skill does. Use this skill when the user wants to [specific use case].\n---\n\n# My API Skill\n\nInstructions for how to use this skill.\n\n## Commands\n\n| Command | Args | Description |\n|---------|------|-------------|\n| `action1` | `<required_arg> [optional_arg]` | What this command does |\n| `action2` | `<arg1> <arg2>` | What this command does |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Typical first step\n2. Then do this\n3. Finally, do this\n\n## Examples\n\n\\`\\`\\`bash\npython3 scripts/main_script.py action1 "argument"\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Output Format\n\nDescribe how output is formatted so OpenCode knows what to expect.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Important notes about usage\n- Environment variables required\nStep 3: Create the Script\nYour script should:\nAccept commands as CLI arguments\nOutput minimal, structured data\nHandle errors gracefully\nLoad credentials from environment variables\nExample Python script structure:\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n"""My API Skill - Brief description."""\n\nimport os\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# Load environment variables from .env file\ndef load_env():\n    env_path = Path(__file__).parent / ".env"\n    if env_path.exists():\n        for line in env_path.read_text().splitlines():\n            line = line.strip()\n            if line and not line.startswith("#") and "=" in line:\n                k, v = line.split("=", 1)\n                os.environ.setdefault(k.strip(), v.strip().strip("\\"\'"))\n\nload_env()\n\n# Get credentials\nAPI_KEY = os.environ.get("MY_API_KEY")\n\ndef action1(arg1, arg2=None):\n    """Perform action 1."""\n    # Your implementation here\n    print(f"Result: {result}")\n\ndef action2(arg1, arg2):\n    """Perform action 2."""\n    # Your implementation here\n    print(f"Result: {result}")\n\nCOMMANDS = {\n    "action1": action1,\n    "action2": action2,\n}\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    if not API_KEY:\n        print("Error: Set MY_API_KEY env var", file=sys.stderr)\n        sys.exit(1)\n    \n    if len(sys.argv) < 2 or sys.argv[1] not in COMMANDS:\n        print(f"Commands: {\', \'.join(sorted(COMMANDS.keys()))}")\n        sys.exit(1)\n    \n    cmd, args = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2:]\n    try:\n        COMMANDS[cmd](*args)\n    except TypeError:\n        print(f"Error: Wrong args for \'{cmd}\'", file=sys.stderr)\n        sys.exit(1)\nStep 4: Create .env.example\n# My API Credentials\n# Get your key from: https://example.com/api-keys\nMY_API_KEY=your_api_key_here\nStep 5: Add requirements.txt (if needed)\nrequests>=2.28.0\nBest Practices\n1. Keep SKILL.md Concise\nOpenCode reads the entire SKILL.md file for context. Keep it focused on:\nWhat commands are available\nWhat arguments they take\nWhat output to expect\nAvoid long prose explanations - use tables and bullet points.\n2. Use Clear Command Names\nGood:\nlist_users\ncreate_task\ndelete_item\nBad:\ndo_thing\nrun\nexecute\n3. Output Minimal, Structured Data\nOpenCode parses your output. Make it easy:\n# Good - structured, minimal\nprint(f"User created: {user_id}")\nprint(f"Name: {name} [id: {id}]")\n\n# Bad - verbose, hard to parse\nprint(f"Successfully created a new user account with the following details: ID={user_id}, Name={name}, Email={email}, Created At={timestamp}...")\n4. Use Consistent ID Formatting\nAlways show IDs in brackets for easy extraction:\nTask Name [abc123]\nAnother Task [def456]\n5. Handle Errors Gracefully\ndef api_call(endpoint):\n    try:\n        response = requests.get(endpoint)\n        response.raise_for_status()\n        return response.json()\n    except requests.RequestException as e:\n        print(f"Error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)\n        sys.exit(1)\n6. Never Commit Credentials\nAlways use:\n.env files for actual credentials (gitignored)\n.env.example files as templates (committed)\nTesting Your Skill\nManual Testing\n# Set up environment\ncd skills/my-api-skill/scripts\ncp .env.example .env\n# Edit .env with real credentials\n\n# Test commands\npython3 main_script.py action1 "test"\npython3 main_script.py action2 "arg1" "arg2"\nTesting with OpenCode\nCopy your skill to a test project:\nmkdir -p test-project/.opencode/skill\ncp -r skills/my-api-skill test-project/.opencode/skill/\nConfigure credentials:\ncd test-project/.opencode/skill/my-api-skill/scripts\ncp .env.example .env\n# Edit .env\nRun OpenCode:\ncd test-project\nopencode\nTest with natural language:\n> Use my-api-skill to do action1 with "test argument"\nSkill Ideas\nHere are some skills you could create:\nGitHub Manager - Manage issues, PRs, and repositories\nNotion Integration - Create and update Notion pages/databases\nSlack Notifier - Send messages to Slack channels\nAWS Helper - Common AWS operations (S3, Lambda, etc.)\nDocker Manager - Container and image management\nDatabase CLI - Query and manage databases\nCalendar Manager - Google Calendar or Outlook integration\nEmail Sender - Send emails via SMTP or API\nContributing Your Skill\nFork this repository\nCreate your skill in skills/\nTest thoroughly\nSubmit a pull request with:\nClear skill description\nExample usage\nAny special setup instructions\nTroubleshooting\n"Command not found"\nMake sure your script is executable:\nchmod +x scripts/main_script.py\n"Environment variable not set"\nCheck that:\n.env file exists in the scripts directory\nVariable names match exactly\nNo extra spaces around = sign\n"OpenCode doesn\'t use my skill"\nVerify:\nSKILL.md has correct frontmatter with name and description\nSkill is in the correct location: .opencode/skill/your-skill/\nDescription clearly states when to use the skill\nHappy skill building! If you create something useful, consider sharing it with the community.', 'links': ['https://github.com/kedbin/opencode-skills/blob/main/docs/creating-skills.md#start-of-content', 'https://github.com/', 'https://github.com/login?return_to=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fkedbin%2Fopencode-skills%2Fblob%2Fmain%2Fdocs%2Fcreating-skills.md', 'https://gi